%I A400325 #17 Oct 08 2026 09:18:42
%S A400325 4,3,5,7,11,13,13,19,23,29,31,37,37,43,47,53,59,61,67,71,69,79,83,85,
%T A400325 93,101,103,107,109,109,127,131,133,139,149,151,157,163,167,173,179,
%U A400325 181,191,189,197,199,211,223,227,229,229,239,237,251,253,263,269,271,277,277
%N A400325 a(n) is the number of ordered pairs (x,y) modulo prime(n) satisfying the equation of the Lemniscate of Bernoulli (x^2+y^2)^2 = x^2-y^2.
%C A400325 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of integer points that the Lemniscate of Bernoulli contains modulo prime(n).
%C A400325 The rational parametrization of the lemniscate x = (t+t^3)/(1+t^4), y = (t-t^3)/(1+t^4) (see Wikipedia link) is undefined when t^4+1 == 0 (mod p). Now, this equation has four solutions exactly when p == 1 (mod 8), hence the count is p-4 in that case. For all other cases with odd prime p, the count is p.
%C A400325 a(n) = a(n+1) iff (prime(n), prime(n+1)) is a pair of consecutive cousin primes with prime(n) == 5 (mod 8) and prime(n+1) == 1 (mod 8). This occurs for prime(n) = 13, 37, 109, 229, ...
%D A400325 Martin Erickson, Beautiful Mathematics, MAA Spectrum, 2011, problem 1, pp. 151 and 155.
%H A400325 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400325/b400325.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400325 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemniscate_of_Bernoulli">Lemniscate of Bernoulli</a>.
%H A400325 <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/Index_to_OEIS:_Section_Cu">Index to sequences related to curves</a>.
%F A400325 For prime(1) = 2, then a(1) = 4.
%F A400325 For odd primes:
%F A400325   if prime(n) <> 1 (mod 8), then a(n) = prime(n), while
%F A400325   if prime(n) == 1 (mod 8), then a(n) = prime(n) - 4 (see explanation in Comments).
%e A400325 One of each type:
%e A400325 For prime(1) = 2, the four ordered pairs (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1) are solutions, so a(1) = 4.
%e A400325 For prime(2) = 3, only the three ordered pairs (0, 0), (1, 0), (2, 0) are solutions so a(2) = 3 = prime(2).
%e A400325 For prime(7) = 17, thirteen ordered pairs (0,0), (0,4), (0,13), (1,0), (2,6), (2,11), (7,8), (7,9), (10,8), (10,9), (15,6), (15,11), (16,0) are solutions, so a(7) = 13 = prime(7) - 4.
%t A400325 Join[{4}, Map[# - If[Mod[#, 8] == 1, 4, 0] &, Prime[Range[2, 100]]]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 08 2026 *)
%Y A400325 Cf. A064853, A076390, A085565, A353049.
%K A400325 nonn,new
%O A400325 1,1
